1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,378
Last time, we said a model is a machine with knobs

2
00:00:03,378 --> 00:00:04,823
and learning means turning

3
00:00:04,823 --> 00:00:06,490
those knobs to the right spot.

4
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:08,789
But what does "right" even mean?

5
00:00:08,789 --> 00:00:10,312
How does the machine know

6
00:00:10,312 --> 00:00:12,017
this turn beat the last one?

7
00:00:12,367 --> 00:00:14,818
The trick is to give its mistakes a score.

8
00:00:15,168 --> 00:00:16,827
That score is called the loss

9
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:18,200
and the rule for working

10
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:19,802
it out is the loss function.

11
00:00:19,802 --> 00:00:21,003
That's today's topic.

12
00:00:29,333 --> 00:00:31,219
Every time the model guesses

13
00:00:31,219 --> 00:00:33,576
it lands a bit too high or too low.

14
00:00:33,576 --> 00:00:35,058
That gap is the error.

15
00:00:35,408 --> 00:00:38,037
Roll all those errors up into one number

16
00:00:38,037 --> 00:00:39,352
and that's the loss:

17
00:00:39,352 --> 00:00:41,192
a score for how wrong it is.

18
00:00:41,542 --> 00:00:43,722
It's the opposite of a test score:

19
00:00:43,722 --> 00:00:44,684
lower is better

20
00:00:44,684 --> 00:00:46,736
and zero means not wrong at all.

21
00:00:47,086 --> 00:00:48,265
Kind of like golf

22
00:00:48,265 --> 00:00:50,346
where fewer strokes is better.

23
00:00:50,696 --> 00:00:52,981
Now the machine can check itself.

24
00:00:52,981 --> 00:00:55,473
If a turn lowers the score, keep it.

25
00:00:55,473 --> 00:00:56,927
If not, turn it back.

26
00:00:57,277 --> 00:00:59,654
So training is just turning the knobs

27
00:00:59,654 --> 00:01:02,224
again and again to push that score down.

28
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:08,808
Remember those twelve made-up taxi rides?

29
00:01:08,808 --> 00:01:10,318
Distance along the bottom

30
00:01:10,318 --> 00:01:11,344
fare up the side.

31
00:01:11,694 --> 00:01:13,800
The model's still that straight line

32
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:14,678
with two knobs:

33
00:01:14,678 --> 00:01:16,901
starting fare and price per kilometer.

34
00:01:17,251 --> 00:01:21,058
First try: a $5 starting fare and $2 a kilometer.

35
00:01:21,058 --> 00:01:23,233
The whole line sits too low.

36
00:01:23,583 --> 00:01:25,825
Take the 5-kilometer ride:

37
00:01:25,825 --> 00:01:28,757
it cost $23, the model guessed $15

38
00:01:28,757 --> 00:01:30,309
so it's off by $8.

39
00:01:30,309 --> 00:01:31,775
That's the error.

40
00:01:32,125 --> 00:01:33,260
Now square it:

41
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:35,206
eight times eight is 64.

42
00:01:35,206 --> 00:01:36,989
That's the red square.

43
00:01:37,339 --> 00:01:39,418
Do that for all twelve rides

44
00:01:39,418 --> 00:01:40,606
take the average

45
00:01:40,606 --> 00:01:42,610
and this setting scores 78.

46
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,258
Second try: $4 to start and $3.50 a kilometer.

47
00:01:46,258 --> 00:01:48,696
Too steep, so short rides come out

48
00:01:48,696 --> 00:01:50,345
low and long ones high.

49
00:01:50,695 --> 00:01:52,027
If we skip the squaring

50
00:01:52,027 --> 00:01:53,590
and just average the errors

51
00:01:53,590 --> 00:01:55,559
the lows and the highs cancel out.

52
00:01:55,909 --> 00:01:57,885
The average is only −$0.33

53
00:01:57,885 --> 00:02:01,078
so it looks like it's barely wrong at all.

54
00:02:01,428 --> 00:02:02,365
So we square.

55
00:02:02,365 --> 00:02:04,168
Everything turns positive

56
00:02:04,168 --> 00:02:05,610
and nothing cancels.

57
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:08,372
And big misses cost way more:

58
00:02:08,372 --> 00:02:10,118
off by two costs four

59
00:02:10,118 --> 00:02:12,280
but off by eight costs 64.

60
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:14,135
Square, then average

61
00:02:14,135 --> 00:02:16,317
and this setting scores 13.4.

62
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:18,516
This is called mean squared error

63
00:02:18,516 --> 00:02:21,149
and it's one of the most common loss functions.

64
00:02:21,499 --> 00:02:25,028
Third try: $10 to start, $2.50 a kilometer.

65
00:02:25,028 --> 00:02:28,393
The squares are tiny, and it scores 2.19.

66
00:02:28,743 --> 00:02:30,500
The third has the lowest score

67
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,257
so it's the best of the three.

68
00:02:32,607 --> 00:02:34,341
But that's only three tries.

69
00:02:34,341 --> 00:02:36,385
Score every setting of both knobs

70
00:02:36,385 --> 00:02:38,490
and you get a landscape of scores.

71
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:40,292
Starting fare along the bottom

72
00:02:40,292 --> 00:02:41,792
price per kilometer up the side

73
00:02:41,792 --> 00:02:42,953
and the darker the color

74
00:02:42,953 --> 00:02:43,969
the higher the score.

75
00:02:44,319 --> 00:02:45,429
Our three tries:

76
00:02:45,429 --> 00:02:46,887
the first way up high

77
00:02:46,887 --> 00:02:48,483
the second halfway down

78
00:02:48,483 --> 00:02:49,593
the third nearly

79
00:02:49,593 --> 00:02:51,536
at the bottom of the valley.

80
00:02:51,886 --> 00:02:54,460
The best is $10.20 to start

81
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:56,558
and $2.54 a kilometer.

82
00:02:56,558 --> 00:02:57,988
It scores 1.97.

83
00:02:58,338 --> 00:02:59,511
It's the best setting

84
00:02:59,511 --> 00:03:01,131
and the whole job of training

85
00:03:01,131 --> 00:03:02,751
is finding that lowest point.

86
00:03:03,101 --> 00:03:04,956
Here's something easy to miss:

87
00:03:04,956 --> 00:03:06,996
even the lowest point isn't zero.

88
00:03:07,346 --> 00:03:08,631
Real fares bounce around

89
00:03:08,631 --> 00:03:09,916
with traffic and detours

90
00:03:09,916 --> 00:03:12,164
so no straight line could nail every ride.

91
00:03:12,514 --> 00:03:14,374
Training isn't chasing zero.

92
00:03:14,374 --> 00:03:16,366
It's getting as low as it can.

93
00:03:20,467 --> 00:03:21,576
Here's the thing:

94
00:03:21,576 --> 00:03:23,989
the model has no idea what a taxi is.

95
00:03:23,989 --> 00:03:25,620
All it sees is the score.

96
00:03:25,970 --> 00:03:28,561
The way you score it shapes how it learns.

97
00:03:28,561 --> 00:03:30,350
Because we squared the errors

98
00:03:30,350 --> 00:03:32,263
it's terrified of big mistakes.

99
00:03:32,613 --> 00:03:34,349
It would rather be a little off

100
00:03:34,349 --> 00:03:36,532
on every ride than way off on just one.

101
00:03:36,882 --> 00:03:38,691
So when you pick a loss function

102
00:03:38,691 --> 00:03:39,877
you're really telling

103
00:03:39,877 --> 00:03:41,460
the model what "good" means.

104
00:03:45,567 --> 00:03:47,636
Big language models like ChatGPT

105
00:03:47,636 --> 00:03:49,382
get a score in training too

106
00:03:49,382 --> 00:03:50,934
just not for taxi fares.

107
00:03:51,284 --> 00:03:53,354
Their job is to guess the next word

108
00:03:53,354 --> 00:03:56,251
like what comes after "Twinkle, twinkle, little."

109
00:03:56,601 --> 00:03:58,500
The score checks how sure the model

110
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:00,073
was about the real next word.

111
00:04:00,073 --> 00:04:02,135
The less sure, the bigger the penalty.

112
00:04:02,485 --> 00:04:05,527
If it was 90% sure of the right word

113
00:04:05,527 --> 00:04:07,133
the penalty's tiny.

114
00:04:07,133 --> 00:04:10,091
Only 10% sure, and it's way bigger.

115
00:04:10,441 --> 00:04:11,713
This kind of scoring

116
00:04:11,713 --> 00:04:13,240
is called cross-entropy.

117
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:15,339
The name is all you need for now.

118
00:04:15,689 --> 00:04:18,107
In 2023, Meta tracked that score while

119
00:04:18,107 --> 00:04:20,144
training a model called Llama 2.

120
00:04:20,494 --> 00:04:22,750
It read 2 trillion tokens of text.

121
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:25,536
Tokens are roughly words or bits of words.

122
00:04:25,886 --> 00:04:26,764
And even then

123
00:04:26,764 --> 00:04:28,587
the score was still falling

124
00:04:28,587 --> 00:04:29,802
nowhere near flat.

125
00:04:30,152 --> 00:04:32,738
A big model has tens of billions of knobs

126
00:04:32,738 --> 00:04:35,009
we have two, but the job's the same:

127
00:04:35,009 --> 00:04:36,397
find the lowest point.

128
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,652
So, a loss function is how

129
00:04:39,652 --> 00:04:41,674
you score the model's mistakes

130
00:04:41,674 --> 00:04:43,022
and lower is better.

131
00:04:43,372 --> 00:04:44,566
And training is just

132
00:04:44,566 --> 00:04:45,999
pushing that score down.

133
00:04:46,349 --> 00:04:47,740
But with tons of knobs

134
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:49,258
that landscape's way too

135
00:04:49,258 --> 00:04:50,712
big to see all at once.

136
00:04:50,712 --> 00:04:53,241
So which way do you turn to go downhill?

137
00:04:53,591 --> 00:04:55,822
That's where gradient descent comes in.

138
00:04:55,822 --> 00:04:56,852
It's up next time.
